WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.160
We all make mistakes,
we're all human,

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.320
and it really is time now

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.280
to find that forgiveness
for yourself,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.720
otherwise you're going to stay
stuck in a cycle of blame,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.160
guilt, shame
and self-punishment, and I know

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.000
from personal experience
that is hell on Earth.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.800
So I woke up that day
and I was really excited

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.440
because it was our school
sports day that afternoon.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.320
I really thought I was in with
a chance of getting a medal.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.360
I walked over to
the runners' box

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.920
and I picked the javelin up,
I took a really deep breath...

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.160
...and headed towards
my friend Sammy.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.880
I just collapsed onto my knees
with my head in my hands

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.680
saying, "Oh, my God,
oh, my God."

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.800
I just couldn't process
what was happening.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.440
The accident was all over
the news, all over the media,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.120
and I think my family wanted to
try and protect me from that.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.040
But nobody really knew
what to do,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.640
and so I was really
sort of left

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.240
to get on with things
on my own.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.360
I already had
a very difficult relationship

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.200
with my stepfather, and on the
way back from Sammy's funeral,

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.600
he said that...

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.200
And so not only did I not
get any professional help

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.720
to come to terms with things,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.120
I couldn't even talk about it
at home.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.840
I just felt like I had to
pretend to everyone

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.520
that I was OK,
when really I was feeling

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.960
all of this mental
and emotional turmoil.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.000
After that therapy,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.400
it was like a weight had been
lifted off my shoulders.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.280
In the moment when I realised
that I'd done nothing wrong,

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.560
I also realised all the pain
and the suffering

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.840
that I had created for myself.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.000
When I talk to people
about self-forgiveness,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.600
I've never experienced
such pain in a room.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.880
It's far greater than
when I talk to people

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.280
about forgiving others.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.120
Self-forgiveness cuts to
the core of our identity.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.040
People often go on journeys
of self-discovery,

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.600
and Lis Cashin's
a very good example of that.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.240
It took decades for her to move
to a place of forgiving herself

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.320
and then, in a way, then moving
beyond forgiving herself,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.080
because she realised she was
a child, it was an accident,

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.840
there were adults there

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.240
who should have taken
the entire...

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.760
...responsibility for
what had happened

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.920
on the school playground
that day.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.320
So the accident had happened,
but then it was me

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.000
that had created these
really destructive beliefs

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.640
about myself - that I was evil,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.400
that I deserved
to be punished -

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.360
and I'd gone on
to punish myself

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.440
in many ways over many years.
And when I realised that,

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.120
it was like my heart broke open

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.160
and I developed
self-compassion,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.160
and really needed
to forgive myself

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.360
for hurting myself
for so many years.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.440
MARINA: I think
self-forgiveness is very much

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.520
wrapped up with guilt
and shame,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.200
and this is why I think

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.920
accepting yourself
for who you are

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.120
is critical to everything.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.960
It's about having this broader
perspective on life.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.200
So for instance, in the case
where you may...

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.360
You may have had
a drug addiction in the past

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.840
and caused a lot of damage
to yourself and others.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.920
You may then actually
broaden your perspective

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.880
to look beyond yourself.
Why had this happened?

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.120
It's an addiction.
It's an illness.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.760
And that can really
make a change

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.360
to how you perceive yourself
and how your life progresses.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:59.680
MARINA: By talking to others,

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.200
by exploring other people's
stories that are similar

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.000
to yours.
What happened to them?

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.000
How is it similar?
How is it different?

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:07.560
And then I think

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.560
key to everything
is self-awareness.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.240
Going on a journey
to discover who you are.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.040
Knowing yourself better.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.960
Because if we don't know
ourselves, we become deluded,

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.040
very often, and we create
great damage in the world.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.720
There's a wonderful quote

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:29.360
by the spiritual writer
Richard Rohr. He says...

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.200
LIS: So now I make it
my mission every day

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.160
to really practise
love and self-care.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.440
I meditate every day,

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.320
I make sure I get out in nature
or exercise.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.280
I have a really brilliant
support network of friends now,

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.520
and family. I'll reach out
for professional support

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:56.800
if I feel I still need it.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.200
And now the thoughts
I have towards myself

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.920
are much more loving,
and accepting, and forgiving.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.680
Some people have said that
self-forgiveness is selfish.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.840
I don't think
that's the case at all.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.280
Because if you can't
forgive yourself,

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.200
you are central to your drama,

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.000
and you're probably telling
everyone about it all the time.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.000
Or if you're not,
it's in your head all the time.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.800
Self-forgiveness
is about making peace with

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.560
things you've done
which you cannot change.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.440
And also it's about accepting

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.680
that you are
a fallible human being

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.480
just like the rest of
the human race.

